  "id": "Compound::DB00352 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Tioguanine appears to be a potential reagent in the treatment of hematologic cancer through a mechanism involving cytopenia, which is a systemic reduction in blood cell counts. This pathway is mediated by both Dasatinib and Imatinib, which are tyrosine kinase inhibitors, and Thiotepa, a chemotherapy agent, all of which are classified as organonitrogen heterocyclic compounds. The evidence suggests that Tioguanine may induce cytopenia, which in turn is linked to the treatment of hematologic cancer, possibly through the activation of downstream pathways that are dependent on these compounds. The shared lowest common ancestor, Tioguanine and the respective drugs, underscores a common pharmacological class, suggesting a potential mechanism of action that involves the modulation of blood cell production or function. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Tioguanine in influencing the systemic effects that may contribute to the therapeutic effect on hematologic cancer.",
